# Lucas C.S. <lucascs@protonmail.com> (2026-11-04)
# boost 1.92 is masked until it has been tested here. ::gentoo ships
# 1.92.0-r1 unkeyworded on purpose while its consumer tracker (bug 983599) is
# open, and the subslot change from 0/1.91.0 to 0/1.92.0 rebuilds every
# package built against boost (about 77 on a desktop host). The python3_15
# copy of 1.91.0-r3 stays available, so masking 1.92 costs nothing on 3.15-only
# systems. To test it, unmask it locally (package.unmask). Lift this entry once
# the rebuild has been tested here, or once ::gentoo keywords 1.92.
=dev-libs/boost-1.92.0-r1

# Lucas C.S. <lucascs@protonmail.com> (2026-10-05)
# Upstream abandoned. Kagi ended Orion for Linux on 2026-10-02 and ships no
# further updates, security fixes included; its own advice is not to use the
# Linux beta as a main browser:
#   https://blog.kagi.com/update-orion-linux-windows
# The bundled WebKit is therefore frozen at the April 0.3.0 bundle, in a
# program whose whole job is to render untrusted content.
#
# icu-compat and libjxl-compat exist only to satisfy orion-bin's Flatpak ABI;
# nothing else in the tree depends on them, so they go with it.
#
# Kagi promised to open-source the code within 30 days. If that yields a free
# license and a maintained fork, the way back is a from-source package, not
# this -bin. Removal on 2026-11-04.
www-client/orion-bin
dev-libs/icu-compat
media-libs/libjxl-compat

# Lucas C.S. <lucascs@protonmail.com> (2026-10-02)
# The Qt 6.12 family, opt-in only: this overlay's survivor of the 6.12
# mirror (fb3c2d3c7, trimmed in 7d5bb0165). qtdeclarative-6.12.0 is a .0
# release that pins ~dev-qt/qtbase-${PV}. qtwebengine-6.140.0_rc left this
# entry with its ebuild on 2026-11-14, when ::gentoo shipped the final
# 6.140.0: bentoo's copy of it is a release, not a pre-release, and
# ::gentoo's own 6.12.0 block still masks it. qtcanvaspainter-6.12.0 (2026-10-03, bentoo-only, for Qt
# Creator 21's Profiler) pins ~dev-qt/qtbase-${PV} too, so it is reachable
# exactly when the rest of 6.12.0 is; its 6.11.2 sibling stays unmasked.
#
# Today ::gentoo's own 6.12.0 mask (ionen, 2026-10-01) already reaches them --
# measured: with no local unmask, emerge refuses them citing
# /var/db/repos/gentoo/profiles/package.mask. That is exactly why this entry
# exists: it is someone else's mask, and it lifts on someone else's schedule.
# ionen's note says qtwebengine will be "treated separately" from the 6.12
# block, so the RC could become reachable the day that block is rewritten,
# with nothing here deciding it. A pre-release stays masked because bentoo
# says so, not because ::gentoo happens to.
#
# The atoms are deliberately unqualified: Portage stamps them "::bentoo",
# which is where these ebuilds live. ::gentoo's copies stay under its own
# mask. To opt in, the same lines ::gentoo suggests cancel both masks, since
# an unqualified unmask matches every repository:
#
#   dev-qt/*:6/6.12.0
#   dev-qt/qtwebengine:6/6.140.0
#
# Remove each line together with its ebuild: qtdeclarative on 6.12.1 (the
# QTBUG-149607 pick is in that tarball). Its .autoupdate record is held for
# the same reason.
# qtcanvaspainter's line goes when ::gentoo lifts its own 6.12.0 mask.
=dev-qt/qtcanvaspainter-6.12.0*
=dev-qt/qtdeclarative-6.12.0*

# Lucas C.S. <lucascs@protonmail.com> (2026-09-04)
# The GStreamer 1.29 development line is missing 29 security advisories and
# there is no release to move to. 1.29.2 was tagged 2026-06-29; the fixes for
# the 1.28.5 batch (2026-07-08) and the 1.28.6 batch (2026-08-05) merged to
# main afterwards, all carrying milestone 1.29.3 -- a tag upstream has never
# cut. Verified per fix rather than inferred: for each of the 29 merge commits
# the GitLab containing-tag list is empty.
#
# Reading the advisories literally hides this. They declare ranges like
# "gst-plugins-bad < 1.28.6", and 1.29.2 < 1.28.6 is false under version
# ordering, so the development branch looks covered. It is not: the range
# describes the stable line, and the development line has its own milestone.
# A development snapshot is only ahead of stable until the moment it is cut.
#
# Fourteen distinct CVEs, several reachable from untrusted media: heap
# out-of-bounds writes in the DVD subpicture decoder (SA-2026-0071), the
# RFB/VNC source (0063, CVE-2026-59691), the closed-caption converter (0060)
# and the IMA ADPCM decoder (0077, CVE-2026-19387); a stack overflow in DTLS
# certificate verification (0062, CVE-2026-59692); and an authentication
# bypass in WebRTC SDP fingerprint validation (0061, CVE-2026-14935).
#
# 1.28.6 is unaffected by all 76 advisories issued in 2026 -- the highest upper
# bound in the entire series is "< 1.28.6" -- and every package masked below
# ships a 1.28.6 ebuild in this overlay. The older line is the safer one here.
#
# KEYWORDS ~arch was the protection this line was published under, and it is
# not enough. ~arch bounds who can reach the ebuild; it does not stop a system
# that accepts ~arch from selecting it. Measured on the maintainer's machine:
# 17 of these packages were installed at 1.29.2 with no 1.28.6 present. That
# is what promoted this from a note to a mask.
#
# The atom is "=<pkg>-1.29*", not "=<pkg>-1.29.2", and the wildcard is the
# point: it also catches 1.29.3 on the day it appears. That is deliberate.
# A milestone label is not a release, and the tag must be checked against the
# 29 fixes before anything from this line is unmasked. .autoupdate/packages.toml
# carries the matching "hold = true" on all 85 <pkg>@unstable records so the
# applier cannot bump past this entry on its own; release the hold and delete
# this block together, never one without the other.
#
# The atoms are deliberately unqualified -- the opposite of the
# net-libs/nodejs:0::gentoo entry below. Portage rewrites every repo-level mask
# atom as "<atom>::bentoo", which is exactly where these ebuilds live; ::gentoo
# ships no 1.29.x at all, so there is nothing there to mask.
#
# Ask the source before lifting this, never the version number:
#
#   curl -s 'https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/api/v4/projects/1357/repository/tags?search=1.29' | jq -r '.[].name'
#
# A 1.29.3 in that list is the start of the check, not the end of it.
=dev-python/gst-python-1.29*
=media-libs/gst-plugins-bad-1.29*
=media-libs/gst-plugins-base-1.29*
=media-libs/gst-plugins-good-1.29*
=media-libs/gst-plugins-ugly-1.29*
=media-libs/gstreamer-1.29*
=media-libs/gstreamer-editing-services-1.29*
=media-libs/gst-rtsp-server-1.29*
=media-plugins/gst-plugins-a52dec-1.29*
